Falsifies `system_tests::user::tests::a_user_program_cannot_read_a_kernel_address`.

Architecture: aarch64

The defect is the kernel mapping its own constants EL0-readable: one flag, in the one place
the kernel image's sections are mapped at their linked addresses. `Flags::user_rodata()` is
`CAP_USER` alone, so the leaf encoder writes `AP_RO_EL0` where it wrote `AP_RO_EL1`, and
nothing else about the page changes: still not writable, still not executable at either
level. The kernel goes on reading its own `.rodata` because this kernel never sets
`PSTATE.PAN`, which `arch::aarch64::mmu::permit_kernel_access_to_user_pages` already records
as a no-op for exactly that reason.

The sentinel the test reaches for is a `static` in the kernel's `.rodata`, so this is the
page it is about.

**Nothing upstream catches it, and that was checked rather than assumed.** The obvious
candidate is `TCR_EL1.E0PD1`, which would fault an EL0 access to the TTBR1 range whatever the
access permission says and would make this patch a red for the wrong reason. This kernel does
not set it: `TCR_EL1` is written once with `T0SZ/TG0/SH0/ORGN0/IRGN0/EPD0/T1SZ/TG1/SH1/ORGN1/
IRGN1/EPD1/IPS` and nothing else. So the permission bit really is the only thing between EL0
and the kernel's constants here, which is what the test says.

Expected to fail: `assert!(wait_for(|| USER_FAULTS.load(Ordering::Relaxed) > before), "the
user program read a kernel address and was NOT stopped")`.

**The two sibling assertions below it never run**, and that is the honest half. The test goes
on to require a `Permission` fault rather than a `Translation` one, and to require the fault
address to be the sentinel's. Both are about a fault that no longer happens, so the test's
most interesting sentence, the one about permission-versus-translation, is not the one that
catches a kernel mapped user-readable; the fault counter is.

**This is aarch64 only, and the reason is the ISA rather than the effort.** `Flags` is
format-neutral and `crates/paging`'s RISC-V encoder turns `CAP_USER` into the Sv39 `U` bit,
which forbids S-mode access unless `sstatus.SUM` is set. So the same defect on riscv64 does
not produce a kernel that lets userspace read it; it produces a kernel that cannot read
itself, and dies before any test runs. The claim in notes/confinement-claims.md row 21 is
stated "on every ISA" and this evidence is not. See that row.

